-# Dev Lifecycle — Plan → Implement → Test → Document
+# Dev Lifecycle — Context → Ceremony → Plan → Implement → Test → Document
 
-_Delivers a ticket from problem statement to documented, merged code._
-
----
-
-## Intent
-
-The Dev lifecycle is the core delivery workflow. Every ticket — feature, bug fix, chore — runs through it. Its job is to ensure that code is planned against the architecture, implemented step-by-step with verification, tested before merge, and documented before close.
-
-Two operational stages run ahead of Plan: **Context Gathering** captures the ticket's
-intent once (tracker/Confluence/approved design/developer input) as `intent.md`, and
-**Ceremony** is the official start of work (tracker transition + branch checkout).
-They exist so Plan can stay dedicated to planning only. The major blocks below —
-Plan, Implement, Test, Document — are unchanged by this; see Phases for the full
-six-stage breakdown.
-
----
-
-## When to use it
-
-Always. There is no ticket that bypasses this lifecycle. For large or novel tickets, run the Design lifecycle first; `/design publish` creates the Dev tickets when design is approved.
-
-## When NOT to use it
-
-There is no "skip Dev" path. The stages can be run individually (`/dev context`, `/dev ceremony`, `/dev plan`, `/dev implement`, `/dev test`, `/dev document`) or all at once (`/dev`), but the sequence is always the same.
-
----
+The dev skill delivers an authorized ticket or local change through planning,
+implementation, verification and documentation. `/dev <TICKET> <problem>` runs the
+full chain; `/dev context|ceremony|plan|implement|test|document <input>` runs one
+stage only. Reuse existing story state on resume and preserve unrelated work.
 
